[
    {
        "content": "<p>Can Loogle (or any other engine) search for an exact match of a signature with type class constraint(s)?</p>\n<p>For example, the library function <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Option.getDM#doc\">docs#Option.getDM</a> has signature</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Option</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"n\">getDM</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_2</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">u_1</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Pure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">y</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Leaving aside the implicit parameters, suppose that I've forgotten the name of this function and want to search for a function with signature</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">Pure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Option</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">m</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Can Loogle find <code>Option.getDM</code> for me?   If I search Loogle for <code>[Pure ?m] → Option ?α → ?m ?a → ?m ?a</code> then it reports</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">Found</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">6</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">declarations</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mentioning</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Pure</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">and</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Option</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Of</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">these</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">match</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">your</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pattern</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">.</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>So apparently not.  Of course I could hunt through those 6 declarations looking for the one I want, but especially in larger searches that is a bother.  I'd like an exact match to appear at the top.</p>\n<p>The natural-language engines don't seem to do much better.  If I query <a href=\"https://www.leanexplore.com\">LeanExplore</a> with <code>[Pure m] → Option α → m a → m a</code> then the first results are <code>OptionT</code>, <code>OptionT.instInhabitedOfPure</code>, <code>OptionT.lift_pure</code> and other inexact matches.  <a href=\"https://leansearch.net\">LeanSearch</a> similarly reports inexact matches, not the one I want.</p>\n<p>I'd be happy to file a feature request at Loogle, but first want to make sure I'm not missing some existing syntax for performing this search, or some other engine that can handle this.</p>\n<p>Now, what I would <strong>really</strong> like is to be able to search for <code>[Monad m] → Option α → m a → m a</code>, and have the search return <code>Option.getDM</code> as the top hit because it understands that <code>Monad</code> extends <code>Pure</code>.  But in the absence of that, at least an exact search capability would be nice.</p>",
        "id": 627731582,
        "sender_full_name": "Adam Dingle",
        "timestamp": 1790678762
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This seems like a natural question to ask (to me, a mathlib maintainer but not involved in Loogle's development). I'm not sure if there are tricks helping here already, or how feasible this is: let's ask loogle's author.<br>\n<span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"470149\">@Joachim Breitner</span> Can you comment on this? Thank you!</p>",
        "id": 627811370,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1790693740
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><code>?m ?a</code> is a higher order pattern, that’s always hard.</p>",
        "id": 627811696,
        "sender_full_name": "Joachim Breitner",
        "timestamp": 1790693798
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"644391\">@loogle</span> |- Pure _ → Option _ → ?m → ?m</p>",
        "id": 627812058,
        "sender_full_name": "Joachim Breitner",
        "timestamp": 1790693874
